      </div>

        <div id="nordictrack-at-sams" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>NordicTrack at Sam’s Club: Which Model Is It?</h2>
<p>
            "Sam’s Club NordicTrack treadmill" is a common search, and the most useful thing to know is that a
            NordicTrack at Sam’s is usually one of the models sold everywhere else, sometimes under a slightly
            longer name, and occasionally a model that NordicTrack does not sell itself.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Commercial series</h3>
          <p>
            Sam’s lists the Commercial 1250, 1750 and 2450 as the "Commercial Series" 1250, 1750 and 2450. These
            are NordicTrack’s mainstream folding running treadmills. The names have been used for several
            generations, and the figures changed between them: older 1250s had a 20-inch belt and a 300 lb
            limit; the current one has a 22-inch belt and a 400 lb limit. Check that the Sam’s listing gives
            the current figures, and our <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1250-vs-1750/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Commercial 1250 vs 1750</a> comparison explains the difference between the two.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Commercial LE</h3>
          <p>
            The Commercial LE is a retailer model: we found it at Sam’s, Abt, Best Buy and Nebraska Furniture
            Mart, but not on NordicTrack’s own US range. It shares the Commercial 1250’s grade range and top
            speed, swaps the touchscreen for a 7-inch display and a device holder, and its motor rating differs
            between retailers. Retailer models are common and not a bad sign in themselves. They do mean you
            should ask NordicTrack directly what the model is based on, its continuous motor rating, its
            capacity and its warranty, and get the answer in writing. Our <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-at-costco/#exclusive-models" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to judging a retailer-exclusive model</a> sets out the line-by-line check.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">iFIT</h3>
          <p>
            Every NordicTrack and ProForm with a touchscreen is designed around a paid iFIT membership, and
            some retail bundles include a trial period. Find out how long the included membership lasts and what
            the screen does without it; our guides to <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether iFIT is worth it</a> and <a href="/can-you-use-nordictrack-without-ifit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">using a NordicTrack without iFIT</a> cover both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="membership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Membership: the Cost Before the Price</h2>
<p>
            Sam’s Club raised its fees on 1 May 2026: the Club membership went from $50 to $60 a year and Plus from
            $110 to $120, according to news reports at the time; existing members pay the new rate at their next
            renewal. Plus members also earn Sam’s Cash rewards, with the annual cap raised to $750.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are not a member,</strong> the fee is part of the treadmill’s price. Sam’s Club’s
            membership terms include a satisfaction guarantee under which it will cancel the membership and refund
            the current year’s fee if you are unsatisfied, so joining to buy one machine is less of a gamble than
            it sounds, but read the current terms before relying on that.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are a member,</strong> sign in before comparing. Several treadmill listings show
            their price only to signed-in members, and the price a non-member sees, if any, may not be the one
            you would pay. Then compare the delivered price, with any assembly, against the same model at
            NordicTrack, Costco, Best Buy and Amazon.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="returns" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Sam’s Club’s Return Policy and Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            Sam’s Club’s returns page, as it read in search results on 5 November 2026, sets out its policy in
            tiers. Most items can be returned at any time unless they are listed as an exception. Electronics and
            major appliances must come back within 90 days; commercial heavy equipment and motorsports items
            within 30 days; and mobile phones within 14 days. Opened games and software can only be exchanged.
            Volume purchases cannot be returned except for manufacturer defects or damage.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Treadmills are not named.</strong> On a strict reading, a treadmill is neither an electronic
            device nor a major appliance and falls under the "return at any time" rule. But a powered <a href="/best-treadmills-with-screens/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>treadmill
            with a touchscreen</a> could reasonably be treated as electronics, and Sam’s says to check each item’s
            description page for its specific terms. Read the return section of the treadmill’s own listing
            before you buy, and if it says nothing, ask Member Services and keep the answer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How a return works.</strong> Sam’s says items bought in a club cannot be shipped back, and
            that online purchases can generally be returned to any US club. For a treadmill that means getting a
            boxed or part-assembled machine of 250 to 310 lb back to the club, unless Sam’s arranges a collection
            for that order. For a full refund you may need to return all the original contents and packaging, so
            keep the box. Our <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill return policies guide</a> compares Sam’s terms with other retailers’, and our guide to <a href="/how-to-disassemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">disassembling a treadmill</a> covers the job if you need to move one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A return is not a trial plan.</strong> A generous policy is a safety net for a machine that
            turns out to be wrong, not a substitute for choosing the right one. Decide the specification first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="delivery-assembly" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Delivery, Assembly and Warranty</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Delivery.</strong> Most of Sam’s Club’s full-size treadmills are sold online and delivered,
            rather than taken from a club floor. Read the delivery line on the listing carefully: kerbside,
            threshold and room-of-choice delivery are very different services for a box that weighs 300 lb, and
            whether shipping is included can depend on the item and your membership tier. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-weigh/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much a treadmill weighs</a> gives boxed weights by type.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Assembly.</strong> If the listing does not include assembly, plan for it: a NordicTrack
            Commercial takes two people a few hours with the manual. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-assemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">assembling a treadmill</a> sets out the job, and its cost belongs in the price comparison with retailers that include it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Warranty.</strong> Sam’s sells the treadmill; NordicTrack or ProForm services it. A Sam’s
            Club help page describes the typical manufacturer warranty on its treadmills as ten years on the
            frame, two years on parts and one year of labour, which matches NordicTrack’s terms on the Commercial
            1250; the 1750 adds a lifetime motor warranty. Register the machine with the manufacturer, keep your
            Sam’s receipt, and note the model and serial numbers. Sam’s may also offer a protection plan at
            checkout; our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warranty guide</a> explains how to judge one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-costco" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Sam’s Club vs Costco for a Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            The two warehouse clubs are the obvious comparison, and for treadmills they overlap more than you might
            expect: both have listed the NordicTrack Commercial 1750, and Costco has listed the 1250.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Membership.</strong> Sam’s Club’s basic membership is $60 a year since May 2026. Costco’s basic membership has been $65 since September 2024. Both have a premium tier.</li>
            <li><strong>Range.</strong> Both carry a short, rotating list. Sam’s is mostly NordicTrack and ProForm; Costco’s range rotates through the year and often includes retailer-specific model numbers.</li>
            <li><strong>Returns.</strong> Both are known for generous policies with exceptions for electronics. Neither names treadmills explicitly in the headline policy, so check the item.</li>
            <li><strong>Price.</strong> On the same model, compare the delivered price on the same day. Neither club is consistently cheaper.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you belong to one club, check that one first; the difference is rarely worth a second membership.
            Our guide to <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-at-costco/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill at Costco</a> covers Costco’s side in detail, and our guides to <a href="/walmart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Walmart treadmills</a>, <a href="/best-buy-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Best Buy treadmills</a> and <a href="/wayfair-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Wayfair treadmills</a> cover the other large retailers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>When to Look</h2>
<p>
            Sam’s Club runs member promotions through the year, and fitness equipment draws the most attention in
            the weeks around Black Friday and the new year. <a href="/black-friday-treadmill-deals-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Black Friday 2026</a> is 27 November. NordicTrack and
            ProForm also run their own sales at those times, so the same machine is likely to be discounted in
            several places at once.
          </p>
          <p>
            Judge any Sam’s Club price against what the same model is actually selling for elsewhere that week,
            not against Sam’s "was" price. Our <a href="/nordictrack-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack Black Friday guide</a> tracks what the main NordicTrack models have sold for, and our guide to the <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best time of year to buy a treadmill</a> sets out the wider cycle.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Recalls to Know About</h2>
<p>
            We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission’s recall database on 5 November 2026. We found
            no recall of any NordicTrack or ProForm treadmill, and no treadmill recall naming Sam’s Club as a
            retailer. iFIT has recalled other NordicTrack and ProForm products: NordicTrack rowing machines in
            October 2025 for a fire hazard, NordicTrack 50 lb iSelect adjustable dumbbells in February 2024, and
            ProForm 50 lb adjustable dumbbells in July 2024. If you are buying any of those through Sam’s or
            elsewhere, check the recall notice first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="checklist" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Before You Buy a Treadmill at Sam’s Club</h2>
<ul>
            <li><strong>Model check.</strong> Does the listing match the current manufacturer model, including belt size and capacity?</li>
            <li><strong>Retailer models.</strong> If it is a model like the Commercial LE, has NordicTrack confirmed the motor, capacity and warranty?</li>
            <li><strong>Price signed in.</strong> What is the member price, and how does the delivered price compare elsewhere the same day?</li>
            <li><strong>Return terms.</strong> What does this item’s page say, and how would you physically return it?</li>
            <li><strong>Delivery level and assembly.</strong> Kerbside, threshold or room of choice; assembly included or not?</li>
            <li><strong>iFIT.</strong> How long is any included membership, and what does the screen do without one?</li>
            <li><strong>Membership cost.</strong> If you are joining for this, have you added the fee?</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are unsure how much treadmill you need, start with our guide to <a href="/how-much-should-you-spend-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much to spend on a treadmill</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Treadmill at Sam’s Club</h2>
          <p>Warehouse-club treadmill purchases go wrong in predictable ways. These four matter most.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming the name means the current model</h3>
              <p>NordicTrack has used names like Commercial 1250 for several generations with different belts, motors and weight limits. Check the belt size and capacity on the Sam’s listing against the current manufacturer figures before buying.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Comparing a non-member price</h3>
              <p>Several Sam’s treadmill listings show their real price only when you sign in. Comparing the price a non-member sees, or a crossed-out "was" price, with another retailer’s delivered price gives the wrong answer.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Relying on "return at any time" without reading the item</h3>
              <p>Sam’s general policy does not name treadmills, and electronics have a 90-day limit. Read the item page or ask Member Services, keep the box, and plan how you would get a 300 lb machine back to a club.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Forgetting iFIT and assembly in the price</h3>
              <p>A touchscreen NordicTrack is designed around a paid iFIT membership, and assembly may not be included. Add both to the Sam’s price before comparing it with a retailer that includes them.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Sam’s Club is a sensible place to buy a NordicTrack or ProForm treadmill if
          you are a member and the signed-in price beats the delivered price elsewhere.</strong> Match the listing
          to the current model, confirm any retailer-only model such as the Commercial LE with NordicTrack, and
          read the item’s own return terms, because Sam’s policy does not name treadmills.
          </p>
          <p>
            Compare it with <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-at-costco/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">Costco</a> and the other channels in our <a href="/treadmills-for-sale/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmills for sale guide</a> before you order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="ed-more not-prose">
          <div class="ed-more-k">Keep reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/buying-a-treadmill-at-costco/">Buying a Treadmill at Costco</a></li>
            <li><a href="/treadmills-for-sale/">Treadmills for Sale</a></li>
